\begin_layout Section
Selecting Boot-up Options
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When you launch Tails,
 you will be presented with a menu of options for configuration including Language and keyboard layout.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Persistent Storage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The option for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Persistent Storage
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 allows you to setup up a dedicated encrypted partition where files can be saved across Tails sessions.
 Saving files to a separate encrypted flash drive offers more flexibility and security,
 however Tails can save settings like WiFi passwords or custom installed software to the persistent storage.
 Setting up persistent storage is a trade off of security in exchange for convenience.
 If your Tails session is accessed remotely by an attacker,
 stored settings could be used to help identify you.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Administration Password
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Additional Settings
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 there is an option for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Administration Password
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 when enabled,
 this allows you to set an admin or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

root
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 password for your session,
 and run a terminal with admin privileges.
 Admin privileges can be used to install additional software do other customizations to your Tails session you would otherwise be locked out of.
 When you restart Tails,
 the Admin password is disabled and reset.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the Administration password is another security trade off,
 one that comes with bigger risks than persistent storage.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When an administration password is unset,
 it is much more difficult for an attacker to potentially install malware or otherwise tamper with your Tails session.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 one of the ways Tails provides anonymity is by offering a standardized sandbox,
 one that is indistinguishable from anyone else running Tails to any attacker able to collect identifying information about your machine.
 Installing custom software breaks that standardization and makes your Tails USB recognizable as a unique instance of Tails,
 which could be used to help identify you.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 extra software presents additional potential vectors for attack.
 The software bundled with tails is audited.
 Bugs that can be exploited by an attacker are still found sometimes,
 but the level of scrutiny is fairly high,
 driving down the risk of exploitable bugs being present,
 and making any bugs that are found more valuable and therefore more less likely to be used on a regular basis.
 Any extra software you install may or may not have the same level of auditing and scrutiny.
 Even if it is well audited,
 all software has bugs,
 and adding software with very few bugs is still more potentially exploitable bugs than existed before.
 
\end_layout

